{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for the kernel key-rotation and signed-checkpoint integration at exact clean head 264a132360551c4998a1cd582fe6061735e737dd on task/kernel-key-rotation-integration.\n\nGates at this head: go vet ./... clean; go test ./... green (kernel 174s); go test -race ./internal/kernel/... ./cmd/gs/... green; go build ./... ok; git diff --check clean; checkout clean at the exact head; current main c95601dade869ec53896cad4b3d8cbbced471cbb is an ancestor; every commit carries a Rests-On trailer.\n\nWHAT HOLDS. The reserved rotation envelope is disjoint from the ordinary event envelope and is canonical: parseRotationMessage requires the marker, exactly one Descriptor line, version 0, a valid SSH key, and a deterministic CBOR round-trip. Rotate refuses a no-op rotation to the current key, signs with the current key, verifies that signature before UpdateRef, and CAS-retries. Both scan paths carry the key forward, and refuse a rotation to the key already current. The substantive fix is real: submit now verifies against log.sequencerPublicKey rather than desc.SequencerPublicKey. Depth counts rotations while Events does not, and a test pins that (depth 3, events 2). TestRetiredSequencerKeyIsRefusedAfterRotation is not vacuous: it proves submission under the retired key fails with a sequencer-signature error, that the ref does not move, and that a commit force-appended with the retired key is named by a full Verify. The continuation case named in the request works: checkpoint before the rotation, rotation in the suffix, a restarted reader consumes the checkpoint and a later resident delta uses the successor. The reference's rotation paragraph states the recovery limits honestly, including that whoever holds a compromised current key can rotate before the legitimate operator does.\n\nMUST FIX, one finding: a rotation inside a checkpointed prefix permanently disables the checkpoint restart cache, silently.\n\nwriteCheckpoint stores Depth: log.Verification.Depth, which counts rotation commits, alongside Events, which does not. validateCheckpoint's first guard refuses any checkpoint where stored.Depth != len(stored.Events). So the first checkpoint written at or after a rotation is unusable, and because every later checkpoint also spans that rotation, every subsequent cold restart falls back to a full audit for the life of the log. writeCheckpoint does not refuse to write it, so the resident keeps rewriting a snapshot it can never read.\n\nReproduced at this exact head in a scratch worktree with a probe test: submit one event, rotate, submit a second event, load once with sequencer custody, then load cold. Both report checkpoint=false depth=3 events=2 - the restart fell back to a full scan.\n\nThis is not a correctness or trust defect. The fallback is the ordinary full audit and nothing is accepted that the full audit would refuse. It is a silent loss of the property main merged two commits ago. spike/README.md still presents restart acceleration with the measured 3.71x and says only that any checkpoint failure falls back to the ordinary full audit; docs/reference.md says a non-descendant checkpoint is only a cache miss. Neither says that one rotation ends checkpointing permanently, so a reader of those pages would not know.\n\nAny of three closes it, and the choice is yours. (a) Carry rotations in the checkpoint, so Depth reconciles and validateCheckpoint advances sequencerPublicKey across the prefix instead of trusting desc.SequencerPublicKey; note that loadCheckpoint also verifies the checkpoint commit against desc.SequencerPublicKey, so the successor has to be resolved before that check, not after. (b) Refuse to write an unusable checkpoint - return ErrNoUsableCheckpoint when Depth != len(Events) - and say plainly in README and reference that rotation ends checkpoint acceleration. (c) Keep the behaviour and state it, with a test pinning it, so the limit is recorded rather than discovered. Whichever you choose, the documentation sentence is the part I will look for.\n\nNOT FINDINGS, recorded so you know they were checked. I looked for a signature weakening in the checkpoint path, since validateCheckpoint does not re-verify per-commit sequencer signatures across the prefix. I could not construct one: validateCheckpoint binds every cached event to the actual commit at that position, and the only prefixes that validate are rotation-free, so the key current across the prefix is the same one the checkpoint commit itself is verified against. Separately, gofmt reports spike/internal/nexus/session_privacy_test.go unformatted; that is pre-existing on main and not yours.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ead5085457e609ab1ee553d781d448733353f9a0","head":"264a132360551c4998a1cd582fe6061735e737dd","verdict":"changes-requested"}}